# Anchoring policy (C10). An unanchored entry like `foo/` matches at EVERY
# depth, so a build/scratch directory name silently swallows any source
# directory that happens to share it. Every entry below that names a
# specific ROOT artifact carries a leading slash; only genuinely global
# patterns (build output, local tool state) are left unanchored.

/target

# Local Claude Code state. The project's CHECKED-IN customizations live
# under `.claude/agents/` and `.claude/skills/` (see CLAUDE.md's "Agent
# skills" table), so ignore the CONTENTS and re-include those two subtrees.
# A blanket `.claude/` cannot be negated — git will not re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded — and it had already eaten
# `agents/sim-diagnostics.md` and `skills/sim-analysis/`: both documented in
# CLAUDE.md, neither in the repo. Same failure as `/diagnostics/` below.
**/.claude/*
!**/.claude/agents/
!**/.claude/skills/

# Root-level artifacts, not name patterns. Anchored so a future
# `crates/*/demos/` or `.../src/reference/` is not swallowed.
/profile.json.gz
/demos/
/reference/
/EEPROM.DAT
/fixtures/debug_adaptive/wanaka_diag.json

# Root-level diagnostics DUMP dir only (`rs_cam_cli`'s JSON export).
# Unanchored, this also swallowed `crates/rs_cam_core/src/diagnostics/`,
# which is how `adapters/from_generation.rs` was written, committed
# against (mod.rs declares it) and yet never landed in the repo — HEAD
# did not compile from a clean clone. Keep the leading slash.
/diagnostics/
